[Event "Thinking Bridge - Balancing"]
[Site ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "1"]
[SkillPath "competitive_bidding/balancing"]
{Kantar TB1-147}
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "E"]
[Vulnerable "EW"]
[Deal "N:AQT872.T54.AQ.Q2 J.AKJ9.K97.K7543 K943.Q763.T2.AJT 65.82.J86543.986"]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer "N"]
[Contract "4S"]
[Result ""]
{

BIDDING COMMENTARY:  North's jump to 2\S in the balancing seat is strong- not a weak jump overcall.  In this position, it
shows a strong six card suit with 13-15 HCP.  East is not strong enough to get back into the fray and South has enough to
raise to game.

DEFENSIVE COMMENTARY:  West signals with the \H8 at trick one and East continues with the king and jack to squash declarer's
10.  West ruffs the \HQ and returns a diamond.

Play Commentary.  Even if the diamond finesse works (and it is a big favorite to fail),  declarer still needs the club
finesse which figures to work.  If the club finesse works, a diamond can be discarded on a winning club,   Why take two
finesses when only one is needed?   North should clearly rise with the \DA, draw trump and run the \CQ through East.  After
this holds, a club is led to the jack and the \DQ goes off on the \CA.  Just as the Dr. ordered.

Never take two finesses when one does the work of two.
}
[Application "kantar2pbn.py; 0.4.4"]
[BCFlags "1f"]
[Auction "E"]
1C Pass Pass 2S
Pass 4S AP
[Play "E"]
HA

[Event "Thinking Bridge - Balancing"]
[Site ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "2"]
[SkillPath "competitive_bidding/balancing"]
{Kantar TB1-160}
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "E"]
[Vulnerable "None"]
[Deal "N:Q95.JT9732.86.A5 A43.KQ.AKT9.T987 KT76.A654.QJ.K43 J82.8.75432.QJ62"]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer "N"]
[Contract "3H"]
[Result ""]
{

BIDDING COMMENTARY:  When the opponents die out at the one level, chances are that the points are rather evenly divided
between the two partnerships.  Translation:  If you are in the balancing (passout) seat with a long suit, bid it!  North
should expect to find 10-12 HCP in partner's hand.  Partner has a bit more than  expectation and raises to 3\H. North is
thrilled to pass.

DEFENSIVE COMMENTARY:  After cashing the DAK,  East exits with the \CJ.

PLAY COMMENTARY:  As declarer, add your HCP to dummy's. Here, both sides have 20 and East has at least 15 of those 20.  With
four certain losers, the play is to avoid guessing who has the \SJ.  The plan is to force them to lead a spade by throwing
them in with a trump.  Win the \CA, cross to the \HA leading the \HJ to induce a cover, cash the \CK and ruff a club,
stripping clubs, and exit a heart. to East. East must either lead a spade, obviating the spade guess or give you a ruff-
sluff  by leading either minor.  If East elects to give you a ruff-sluff, discard a spade from your hand, the hand that is
shorter in spades, and ruff in partner's hand, the hand that is longer in spades- the technique for handling a ruff and a
sluff in an unequally divided suit.  If you neglect to strip clubs before exiting a heart, East can exit safely with a club
leaving you to guess who has the \SJ-messy, messy.

With an inevitable slow trump loser (\HK),  and a suit you want to force them to lead (spades), strip the side suit(s)
before exiting (triumphantly) with a trump.
}
[Application "kantar2pbn.py; 0.4.4"]
