[Event "Thinking Bridge - Balancing"]
[Site ""]
[Date ""]
[Board "5"]
[SkillPath "competitive_bidding/balancing"]
{Kantar TB1-294}
[West ""]
[North ""]
[East ""]
[South ""]
[Dealer "W"]
[Vulnerable "NS"]
[Deal "N:QT8.QT7.AKQ.T987 A653.J2.JT4.J632 9.K985.8653.KQ54 KJ742.A643.972.A"]
[Scoring ""]
[Declarer "W"]
[Contract "2S"]
[Result ""]
{

BIDDING COMMENTARY:  It is difficult for North-South to get in the bidding. North has the points to make a takeout double of
either 1\S or 2\S, but not the right distribution plus a  likely wasted \SQ. A player who makes a takeout double with
minimum values should be short in the doubled suit (one or two cards) and have four cards in the unbid major. South has the
perfect distribution to double 2\S, but as a non-passed hand, isn't strong enough. South needs 10+ to 11 HCP, minimum, to
double 2\S with this pattern.

DEFENSIVE COMMENTARY:  As North, cash three diamonds and shift to the \C10. As South, hang on to your hearts for dear life
if declarer plays two rounds of spades early on.

Play Commentary: As West, the idea is to trump two hearts in dummy after playing the  SAK. But there's a catch. If you play
the SAK and then concede a heart, if someone started with three spades headed by the queen, that player may win your heart
exit and cash the \SQ, a strong defensive play, removing a precious trump from dummy meaning you can only trump one heart in
dummy. You will lose 6 tricks: 3 diamonds, 2 hearts and the \SQ. The counter is to duck a heart before playing the SAK.  Now
you can trump both hearts in dummy because there are two trumps in dummy, not one.
}
[Application "kantar2pbn.py; 0.4.4"]
